6. Analysis & Observations
Strengths. Growth, margin expansion and falling finance costs all moved in the right direction together. Profit grew almost three times faster than revenue, showing real operating leverage. Leverage is low, so the company has room to borrow for expansion.
Risks and watch points. Working capital is the main drag: about ₹1,400 lakhs is tied up in inventory and receivables. A 69-day cash conversion cycle means growth will consume cash unless collections and stock turns improve. Employee costs are rising about 11% a year and will need to stay below revenue growth to hold margins.
7. Recommendations
1. Cut receivable days from 59 to about 50 through tighter credit terms and early-payment discounts. This would release roughly ₹120 lakhs of cash.
2. Reduce inventory days from 78 to about 65 with better demand planning and slow-moving stock reviews.
3. Prepay part of the long-term loan if surplus cash builds, since interest cover and liquidity are both comfortable.
4. Set a capex budget tied to returns, with each project needing a payback period under four years.
5. Move to monthly management reporting with a rolling 12-month cash forecast.
8. Outlook for FY 2026-27
If the Q4 run-rate of about ₹1,320 lakhs per quarter holds, revenue could reach ₹5,300–5,600 lakhs next year, assuming stable margins. The main sensitivities are raw material prices, customer payment behaviour and wage inflation.
